# shir_nodes/uniq_lines.node — expression-level semantic IR node.
# Collapse runs of EQUAL ADJACENT lines (`uniq`, `uniq -c`, `uniq -s N`).
#
# Small (3 corpus files) but load-bearing: it is half of the canonical
# word-frequency pipeline `sort | uniq -c | sort -nr`, which no backend
# could express. With SortLines it makes that pipeline a COMPOSITION of two
# specified nodes rather than an opaque shell-out.
#
# meaning: uniq_lines skip count s == the lines of `s` with every maximal
#   run of equal lines reduced to one line. `skip` drops the first `skip`
#   CHARACTERS of each line for the COMPARISON only (what `uniq -s N` does —
#   the printed line keeps them). `count` prefixes each surviving line with
#   its run length and a single space, as `uniq -c` prints it.
#
# Deliberately NOT covered: `uniq -d` / `-u` (filters, not a value node —
# they need the run lengths, so they are a later node or a composition),
# `-f N` (skip FIELDS; needs the IFS question that `sort -t` also raises)
# and `-i` (case folding, which is locale-sensitive).
#
# Backends: Perl List::Util uniq-free run collapse, JS filter, Go slice
#   walk, Rust dedup_by
# Fallback: sh2.uniqLines(text, skip, count)
#
# Provenance: sh2perl/docs/fm/TextOps/ShIR_TextOps_SortUniq.thy

node UniqLines
tag "UniqLines"
kind expr
field text: expr
# skip: characters ignored at the START of each line when deciding whether
# two lines are equal (`uniq -s N`). Values beyond a line's length make it
# compare as the empty suffix, which is what uniq does.
field skip: int
# count: prefix each surviving line with its run length and a space
# (`uniq -c`). The count node is NOT a separate node: it is the same run
# walk with a different projection, and splitting it would give two nodes
# the same denotation.
field count: bool
spec: TextOps#uniq_lines_sound
# `uniq -s N` (`skip`): proved as `TextOps#uniq_lines_skip_sound` in
# ShIR_TextOps_SortUniq.thy, with the value written keep-first over the run leader
# (`key_runs (drop N)`): the survivor of a run is the FIRST line, so the skipped
# characters survive in the printed line, which is exactly what `uniq` prints.
# The theorem gives the length bound, the head (keep-first) law and `key_free`
# (no two survivors share a suffix) — the run structure outright. Two boundary
# cases are theorems too: `uniq_lines_skip_zero` (at N = 0 the skip collapse IS
# `drop_runs`) and `uniq_lines_skip_merges` (at N = 1 it is not: ["ab","xb"]
# collapses to ["ab"], which `drop_runs` keeps apart). A `drop_runs`-shaped
# recursion keeps the LAST member of a run, which coincides with keep-first only
# under exact equality — that was the trap, not the relation parameter.
spec: TextOps#uniq_lines_skip_sound
